            answer: ["A", "C", "D"],
            solution: "• As data rate increases, transmission time decreases for a fixed frame size. To satisfy collision detection (≥ 2 × propagation delay), the network length must be reduced. So, option (a) is correct.<br><br>• Propagation delay depends on distance and signal propagation speed. Since distance increases with cable length, delay is directly related, not independent. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br><br>• For correct collision detection, the sender must still be transmitting when a collision signal returns. Hence, minimum frame size ≥ data rate × 2 × propagation delay. So, option (c) is correct.<br><br>• Longer cable increases propagation delay, so collision signals take more time to return. This increases the risk that transmission finishes before detection, causing failure in collision detection. So, option (d) is correct."
