            solution: "When page size is halved, the number of pages required to represent the same logical  address space doubles. Since each page requires one page table entry, the total number of  entries increases. Hence, the page table size per process increases. So, option (a) is  correct.<br>With smaller pages, each TLB entry maps a smaller memory region. This reduces  spatial locality coverage per entry and increases the chance of TLB misses. Therefore, TLB  hit ratio generally decreases, not increases. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br>Internal fragmentation is the unused space within a page. Smaller page size reduces  the maximum possible unused space per page. Hence, internal fragmentation decreases. So,  option (c) is correct.<br>Number of pages = Process size / Page size If page size is halved while process size remains  same, the number of pages doubles. Therefore, the number of pages per process increases.  So, option (d) is correct."
